      "text": "To determine the maximum number of planes that can be uniquely determined by 12 points in space, we can use the combination formula to find the number of ways we can choose 3 points out of 12. The combination formula is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of points, \\( r \\) is the number of points to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial, which is the product of all positive integers up to that number.\n\nIn this problem, we have \\( n = 12 \\) points and we want to choose 3 points to determine a plane. So, we need to calculate \\( \\binom{12}{3} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\( 9! \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( 3! \\):\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]",
